    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "I added the rule that level 4 requires more than 365 days of tenure. The full test suite passes: 14 tests, 0 failures.\n\n**Before my change:** the existing code already capped every owner at level 3, and the new acceptance test (`AcceptanceTest.coreNewOwnerCappedAtLevel3`) was already passing. So this change doesn't alter any API output. It writes the tenure rule into the code and the docs.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`:** I added two constants: level 4 as the tenure level, and 365 as the number of days it requires. The level calculation now lives in `membershipLevel(owner, tenureDays)`. The usual factors still cap at 3: start at 1, plus 1 for an email, plus 1 for no namesakes. An owner at 3 moves to 4 only when tenure is more than 365 days.\n- **`toMembershipLevel(owner)`:** this is what the API response and the audit log use. It passes zero tenure, because a new owner has none. So a new owner with an email and no namesakes is level 3, not 4.\n- **`openapi.yml`:** the `membershipLevel` description now states the 365-day rule. I left `maximum: 3` as it is, because the API never returns 4 today.\n\n**Not yet in place:**\n- **Tenure isn't tracked.** Owners have no creation timestamp, so nothing passes a real tenure value yet, and no owner can reach level 4. I didn't measure tenure from `registrationDate`, because clients can set it to a past date. That would let a brand-new owner reach level 4, which breaks the rule.\n- **Household size isn't part of the calculation.** The spec mentions a 3-member household, but the current code doesn't use it, and I didn't add it.",
